Build
-----
  gcc -std=c89 -pedantic -W -Wall -Wpointer-arith -Wwrite-strings \
      -Wstrict-prototypes asm.c -o asm
  gcc -std=c89 -pedantic -W -Wall -Wpointer-arith -Wwrite-strings \
      -Wstrict-prototypes emu.c -o emu
Both programs compile with no errors and no warnings with these flags.

Usage
-----
  ./asm file.asm            writes file.obj and file.lst
                            (on errors: writes only file.log)
  ./emu file.obj [words]    writes file.log (trace, final registers,
                            memory dump of [words] words, default 64)

------------------------------------------------------------
ASSEMBLER (asm.c)
------------------------------------------------------------
Claimed as done:
 - Two pass assembler. Pass 1 (pass1) builds the symbol table, pass 2
   (pass2) emits code. These are two separate functions and the source
   file is re-read with rewind() between them.
 - Instruction table (inst_table) with mnemonic, opcode, whether an
   operand is expected, and whether the operand is a branch offset.
 - Encoding: (operand << 8) | opcode. Operands must fit in 24 bits,
   otherwise an error is reported.
 - Label handling: forward references, label on its own line, label
   followed directly by a mnemonic (label:ldc 5), branch displacement
   for label operands of br, brz, brlz and call.
   A plain number given to a branch instruction is used as the offset.
 - Numbers: decimal, hexadecimal (0x) and octal, via strtol, with a
   check that the whole token was consumed.
 - data pseudo instruction and the SET pseudo instruction.
 - Errors found and reported (with line numbers) in one run, written to
   <name>.log:
     duplicate label, undefined label, invalid label name,
     unknown mnemonic, invalid number, missing operand,
     unexpected operand, extra text at end of line,
     SET without a label, operand does not fit in 24 bits,
     label too long, symbol table overflow, line too long
     (only when code is cut off by the 255 character line limit; a
     long comment is simply ignored).
 - Warning (printed on the terminal): label defined but never used.
 - Binary object file (<name>.obj), code starts at address 0.
 - Listing file (<name>.lst) in the preferred format: address, machine
   word and mnemonic with operand, plus a line "address label:" for
   every label.
 - Lines are read with fgets and split with strtok.

Known limitations (not claimed):
 - Object words are written as native int (little-endian on x86).
 - Mnemonics are case sensitive (HALT and SET are upper case).
 - Labels are looked up by linear search.
 - The two passes duplicate some parsing code; they are not one routine.

------------------------------------------------------------
EMULATOR (emu.c)
------------------------------------------------------------
Claimed as done:
 - Loads the binary object file into a 65536 word memory.
 - Fetch, PC increment, decode, execute for all instructions
   (ldc to HALT), with 24 bit sign extension of the operand.
 - Writes a trace of every instruction (PC, A, B, SP, opcode, operand),
   the final registers and a memory dump of the requested size.
 - Detects errant programs: illegal opcode, PC outside memory,
   memory read or write outside memory (this includes a bad SP when it
   is used by ldl/stl), shift count outside 0..31, and a step limit of
   100000 instructions for infinite loops.

Known limitations (not claimed):
 - SP itself is not range checked; only the memory accesses made with it.
 - Writing into the code area is allowed.

